import{_ as o,C as a,o as n,c as r,j as t,a as i,E as h,a6 as d}from"./chunks/framework.CCKCzdWs.js";const k=JSON.parse(`{"title":"Watch a running test","description":"Watch the page of a Playwright test while it runs on your machine, from VS Code, a JetBrains IDE, Piwi Picker's Runs window or the desktop app: up to ten images a second, through its next tests and stops.","frontmatter":{"title":"Watch a running test","description":"Watch the page of a Playwright test while it runs on your machine, from VS Code, a JetBrains IDE, Piwi Picker's Runs window or the desktop app: up to ten images a second, through its next tests and stops.","lang":"en-US","head":[["link",{"rel":"canonical","href":"https://piwitests.dev/features/watch-running-test.html"}],["meta",{"property":"og:url","content":"https://piwitests.dev/features/watch-running-test.html"}],["meta",{"property":"og:title","content":"Watch a running test | Piwi for Playwright"}],["meta",{"property":"og:description","content":"Watch the page of a Playwright test while it runs on your machine, from VS Code, a JetBrains IDE, Piwi Picker's Runs window or the desktop app: up to ten images a second, through its next tests and stops."}]]},"headers":[],"relativePath":"features/watch-running-test.md","filePath":"features/watch-running-test.md","lastUpdated":1794474906000}`),c={name:"features/watch-running-test.md"};function l(w,e,u,g,p,f){const s=a("Needs");return n(),r("div",null,[e[0]||(e[0]=t("h1",{id:"watch-a-running-test",tabindex:"-1"},[i("Watch a running test "),t("a",{class:"header-anchor",href:"#watch-a-running-test","aria-label":'Permalink to "Watch a running test"'},"​")],-1)),h(s,{reporter:"",fixtures:""}),e[1]||(e[1]=d('<p>A test that runs headless on your machine, or in a remote container, shows its page while it runs: up to ten images a second, through its next tests and its stops, and the worker Playwright starts in its place after a failure. Choose a worker in your editor, in Piwi Picker&#39;s Runs window or in the desktop app, and watch it work; <strong>Pause and watch</strong> stops it before its next call too. It needs <code>@piwitests/reporter</code> newer than 0.67.0 and the <a href="/guide/capture-fixtures.html">capture fixtures</a>. Nothing leaves your machine.</p><h2 id="where-to-watch" tabindex="-1">Where to watch <a class="header-anchor" href="#where-to-watch" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Where to watch&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Where</th><th>Open it</th><th>At a stop</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>VS Code</td><td><strong>Piwi: Watch a worker&#39;s test</strong>, or a click on the run&#39;s progress in the status bar: <strong>Piwi: worker 2</strong> beside the editor</td><td>A headless stop of the worker shows there, with <strong>Interact</strong> and the pick on a click, instead of the paused page</td></tr><tr><td>JetBrains IDEs</td><td><strong>Tools → Piwi → Watch a Worker&#39;s Test</strong>: the <strong>Worker 2</strong> tab of the Piwi tool window</td><td>The tab only watches: a headless stop opens the <strong>Paused Page</strong> beside it, for <strong>Interact</strong>, unless that tab holds another stop</td></tr><tr><td>Piwi Picker</td><td><strong>Watch</strong> on a worker&#39;s row of the <a href="./runs-window.html#watch-live">Runs window</a></td><td>The controls show above the view, and the stopped worker scrolls into view</td></tr><tr><td>Desktop app</td><td><strong>Watch</strong> on a running row of its own runs, which opens the <a href="./desktop-runs.html#watch-live">Local runs</a> tray on it</td><td>The tray shows the stopped worker, behind its tab in a run of several</td></tr></tbody></table><p><strong>Pause and watch</strong> is <strong>Piwi: Pause and watch a worker&#39;s test</strong> in VS Code, <strong>Tools → Piwi → Pause and Watch a Worker&#39;s Test</strong> in a JetBrains IDE, and a button beside <strong>Watch</strong> in the Runs window and the desktop app. With several workers to choose from, the editor asks which.</p><h2 id="a-ci-run-of-your-branch" tabindex="-1">A CI run of your branch <a class="header-anchor" href="#a-ci-run-of-your-branch" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;A CI run of your branch&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A CI run of the branch your workspace reads or has checked out shows beside your own runs while it runs, read-only: nothing in your editor or Piwi Picker pauses, steps, holds or shares it.</p><ul><li><strong>VS Code</strong>: the <strong>CI Runs</strong> view of the Piwi panel lists it as <code>CI run #78</code>, its branch and progress beside it, then <strong>Open run #78</strong> and its tests: those running, then the latest that ended, failures first. A running test opens the call it is at, else its spec.</li><li><strong>JetBrains IDEs</strong>: the <strong>CI Run</strong> tab of the Piwi tool window, added while one runs, lists the same rows; a double click opens one.</li><li><strong>The line each running test is at</strong> ends with a plain mark in the CodeLens or inlay color, <code>CI run #78 · worker 2: Click getByRole(&#39;button&#39;)</code>: a CI position, never a stop. The line is the run&#39;s commit&#39;s.</li><li><strong>Piwi Picker&#39;s Runs window</strong> lists the CI runs in progress of the projects your <a href="./extension-connection.html">connection</a> maps, through that instance, with <strong>Open</strong>.</li><li><strong>Watch</strong>, under a running test of a run whose project has the <a href="./live-view.html">live view</a> on, opens its lane in the editor, read-only: the VS Code worker panel or the JetBrains <strong>Worker</strong> tab, named <code>CI run #78 · worker 2</code>, with <strong>Watch</strong> alone, no <strong>Interact</strong> and no pick. It follows the lane through its next tests, and ends with the run, as you hide or close the view, or as the instance closes it, saying why.</li><li><strong>Watch on the dashboard</strong>, beside it, opens the lane&#39;s view on the run&#39;s page: the choice where the editor cannot show it (an older instance, a refused <strong>Watch</strong>, which offers it).</li></ul><p>The editor follows the two newest CI runs of a branch through the connection to the instance it already holds, and Piwi Picker reads the list every 15 seconds while its Runs window is open. While the editor follows a run, the run&#39;s reporter streams the call each test is at. A CI lane&#39;s images come from the team instance, read with your editor&#39;s key: the CI worker paints over the same fields before they leave it, the editor service keeps none, and the watch counts among the instance&#39;s watches of the run for the key&#39;s owner, as a dashboard tab does.</p><h2 id="what-you-see" tabindex="-1">What you see <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-you-see" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What you see&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Each view names the worker by its slot in the run (<code>Worker 2</code>) and says what it does: its running test, the call a Pause waits in, where it stopped, between tests, or starting. Across a <strong>Step</strong>, a <strong>Resume</strong> or between two tests, the last image stays until the next one comes, dimmed in VS Code, the Runs window and the desktop app, the last two marking it <strong>Running…</strong>. A page that does not change sends no new image.</p><p>Watching pauses while the view is out of sight (another tab in front of an editor&#39;s panel or tab, a minimized Runs window) and starts again as it shows. It ends when you turn it off or close the view, or as the run ends.</p><h2 id="beside-the-image" tabindex="-1">Beside the image <a class="header-anchor" href="#beside-the-image" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Beside the image&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Under the image, the editor&#39;s view shows what the pixels cannot, a CI run&#39;s lane included:</p><ul><li><strong>Why it waits</strong>: the call the test is in, how long it has run, how many elements its locator matches and whether the first is enabled, and the last lines of Playwright&#39;s log.</li><li><strong>Events</strong>: console and uncaught errors, failed requests, requests pending long (<code>POST /api/checkout pending 17 s</code>) and the errors the backend logged, newest first.</li><li><strong>Text</strong>: the page as roles and names, its secret fields hidden. Find searches it.</li><li><strong>Filmstrip</strong>: one image per call the test finished; click one to see it larger.</li></ul><p>The text and the filmstrip are read only while open. The view says when an older reporter, or team instance, may send none of them.</p><h2 id="interact" tabindex="-1">Interact <a class="header-anchor" href="#interact" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Interact&quot;">​</a></h2><p>At a stop, <strong>Interact</strong>, beside <strong>Watch live</strong> or <strong>Watch</strong> in each view once the view is on, sends your clicks, the wheel and your keys on the view to the page as a person&#39;s would. The first of them takes control of the stop, so an agent waits until you give it back (<a href="/guide/concepts.html#presence-driving-a-stop">driving a stop</a>). It stays on across the worker&#39;s next stops, where your first click or key takes the stop again. Turning it or watching off, <strong>Give back</strong> or <code>Esc</code> on the view turns it off and gives the stop back, once the input sent before has reached the page. While a pick or a check runs on the page, started there or from its pause bar, <code>Esc</code> goes to the page instead, as its own key: it skips the pick or closes the check. A recording takes no <code>Esc</code> of its own, so there <code>Esc</code> gives the stop back. A <code>Shift</code>+click picks a locator instead. The keys that stay the window&#39;s are listed under <a href="/reference/keyboard-shortcuts.html#piwi-picker-s-runs-window">keyboard shortcuts</a>.</p><h2 id="what-is-sent-and-what-is-kept" tabindex="-1">What is sent, and what is kept <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-is-sent-and-what-is-kept" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What is sent, and what is kept&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The worker takes images only while a view watches it, and sends them to the editor service or the desktop app&#39;s server on your machine, which passes each to the views watching that worker and keeps none. Before an image leaves the worker, password fields and the fields of one-time codes and card numbers are painted over. One view of the page serves every viewer at once: the editor&#39;s, Piwi Picker&#39;s and, for a run <a href="./share-live.html">shared live</a>, the team&#39;s.</p><h2 id="limits" tabindex="-1">Limits <a class="header-anchor" href="#limits" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Limits&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>One worker per editor.</strong> The panel or tab watches one worker, a CI run&#39;s lane included: watching another moves it there.</li><li><strong>The test&#39;s <code>page</code>.</strong> A popup or a second page the test opens is not shown. A download a click on the view starts and a dialog the page opens go to the test as its own would: Playwright deletes the download with the test&#39;s context, and dismisses the dialog unless the test handles it.</li><li><strong>A test&#39;s own screencast.</strong> When the test records its own (a video, a trace with screenshots), the view takes its size and quality; when none can start, it is an image of the page twice a second.</li><li><strong>A heavy page.</strong> A frame over 384 KB gives way to a lighter image of the page, twice a second; when even that is too heavy, the view says so in its place.</li><li><strong>An older reporter</strong> shows a stopped test alone: the Runs window&#39;s row and the desktop app&#39;s tray hold their <strong>Watch</strong>, saying to update it, and the editors offer no Watch for its workers.</li></ul><h2 id="related" tabindex="-1">Related <a class="header-anchor" href="#related" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Related&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><a href="./runs-window.html">Runs window</a>: watch, steer and pick from a window beside the test&#39;s browser.</li><li><a href="./editor-run-control.html">Pause and step from the editor</a>: the controls and the paused page, in the editor.</li><li><a href="./desktop-runs.html">Desktop app runs</a>: the runs the desktop app starts, and its tray.</li><li><a href="./live-view.html">Live view of a run</a>: a CI run&#39;s or a shared run&#39;s page, on the team instance.</li><li><a href="./editor-runs.html">Runs from the editor</a>: your own runs, and the status bar that counts a CI run of your branch.</li></ul>',23))])}const y=o(c,[["render",l]]);export{k as __pageData,y as default};
